---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/advanced/social-networks">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Sociology. It builds on the foundational lesson <em>Society
    and Socialisation</em> and on the intermediate lessons <em>Class and Stratification</em>
    and <em>Race, Ethnicity and Identity</em> — you already know that group membership and
    stratification shape life chances. This chapter adds a third lens: the pattern of ties
    itself, measured and tested.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define degree, clustering, betweenness, eigenvector centrality and bridges — and say what social claim each measure makes;</li>
    <li>explain why weak ties outperform strong ties for novel information, and why brokers beat the merely popular;</li>
    <li>describe the small-world recipe and what it implies for epidemics and rumours;</li>
    <li>untangle contagion from homophily, and say why complex contagion reverses the optimal network shape;</li>
    <li>trace how bonding and bridging social capital turn network structure into unequal opportunity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Classical sociology explained outcomes by attributes — class, education, gender. Network analysis
    adds a second axis: <strong>structure</strong>. The same person embedded in a dense clique
    versus spanning two disconnected groups lives different social lives, receives different
    information, catches different epidemics, and gets different jobs — even with identical
    attributes. The discipline's wager is that many outcomes attributed to individuals or
    institutions are actually properties of the pattern of ties, and that pattern is measurable:
    nodes, edges, degree, density, centrality, clustering — a quantitative language for the social
    world that predates social media by decades and now runs the platforms that reorganise it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="weak ties, small worlds, contagion, structural inequality" widget="equationStep" config={netCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels in order — each pairs a
    structural claim with a social consequence. Watch how the same vocabulary recurs: weak ties
    and small worlds are bridge-and-path statements, contagion turns on clustering, and the
    final panel restates brokerage as inequality. This is one machine, not four separate ideas.
  </p>

  <h2>The vocabulary: measures that carry theory</h2>
  <p>
    Every measure below is a claim about how social life works. <strong>Degree</strong> (number of
    ties) is raw connectivity; its distribution matters more than its average — human networks have
    heavy-tailed degree distributions, so a few high-degree "hubs" dominate flow, which is why
    superspreading is a network fact, not a metaphor. <strong>Clustering</strong> (your friends know
    each other) measures closure — the density that builds trust and enforces norms. <strong>Betweenness
    centrality</strong> counts how often you sit on the shortest path between others: the broker's
    measure. <strong>Eigenvector centrality</strong> weights connections by how well-connected your
    contacts are (the logic behind PageRank). And a <strong>bridge</strong> — the only edge between
    two clusters — is simultaneously the channel for novel information and the point where
    removal splits the network. Keep these in hand; every result below is one of them doing work.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each measure with the claim it carries" widget="match" config={netMeasuresMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the network lens treats the pattern of ties — not just personal
    attributes — as a cause of social outcomes, and the measures above are its instruments.
    Next comes the founding result that made the lens indispensable, built almost entirely
    from one of them: the bridge.
  </p>

  <h2>The strength of weak ties</h2>
  <p>
    Granovetter's 1973 paper began as a survey: how did professional, technical and managerial
    workers in a Boston suburb find their jobs? Of those who found jobs through personal contacts,
    only a minority saw the contact often; most rarely — the decisive tie was an acquaintance. The
    mechanism is structural, not sentimental: close friends are informationally <em>redundant</em>
    (they inhabit your world and know what you know), while acquaintances bridge to clusters whose
    information you cannot otherwise reach. Granovetter deepened the point with <strong>embeddedness</strong>:
    even economic transactions run through social relations — trust is cheaper than contract
    enforcement, which is why hiring (the riskiest economic act firms perform) leans so heavily on
    networks. The idea generalised into the strongest empirical finding of economic sociology:
    labour markets are structured by information flow through ties, which is also exactly why
    network position converts into opportunity — and why unequal networks convert into unequal
    opportunity without anyone intending it (return to this at the chapter's end).
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the founding result's facts" widget="cloze" config={netGranovetterCloze} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "networking means knowing as many people as possible."
  It seems reasonable because degree is the most visible measure and popular people do seem
  advantaged. But the lesson's evidence runs the other way: strong ties are informationally
  redundant, and Burt found it is brokers — people spanning disconnected clusters rather than
  the best-connected — who are promoted faster and paid more. The correct picture: a smaller
  network with the right bridges beats a large clique of overlapping contacts, because
  brokerage buys information your whole cluster cannot see.</p>

  <h2>Small worlds: structure, not friendliness</h2>
  <p>
    Milgram's chain-letter experiments (1967): give a Nebraska resident a letter addressed to a
    Boston stockbroker and instruct them to forward it only to someone they know on a first-name
    basis. Completed chains averaged roughly six intermediaries — "six degrees of separation" —
    though the honest reading includes the selection bias that only about a fifth of chains
    completed, and completed chains are by construction the successful ones. The scientific
    surprise came from Watts and Strogatz (1998), who showed how ordinary the structure is: start
    from a regular lattice (highly clustered, long paths) and rewire a small fraction of edges at
    random. A tiny rewiring probability collapses average path length while clustering barely
    drops — a <strong>small-world network</strong> with both local density and global shortness.
    A few long-range ties (the acquaintance from another city, the job in another country) do
    nearly all the global bridging. Barabási and Albert's later <strong>scale-free</strong> model
    (preferential attachment: the well-connected get more connected) explains the hub structure
    that makes both epidemics and viral content explosive. The lesson generalises: small-world
    structure is why diseases traverse the planet in weeks, why rumours outrun corrections, and
    why network interventions (targeting hubs, cutting bridges) beat population-average ones.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="order the small-world research arc" widget="order" config={netSmallOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> speed and reach come from structure — a few bridges and hubs do
    nearly all the global connecting, which is why diseases and rumours outrun institutions.
    Next, the hardest question in the field: when a behaviour tracks the network, is that
    influence, or just similarity finding similarity?
  </p>

  <h2>Contagion and the homophily problem</h2>
  <p>
    Christakis and Fowler's Framingham-derived analyses reported that obesity, smoking cessation and
    even happiness "spread" through networks — a friend's weight gain raising your own risk by
    measurable percentages, with effects decaying with network distance. The headline claims forced
    the field to confront the central identification problem: <strong>homophily</strong>. People
    choose friends similar to themselves; if similar people share environments, trajectories and
    lifestyles, they will co-move through time with <em>no</em> social influence at all. Untangling
    influence from selection requires either randomised exposure (rare and hard) or models with
    strong temporal assumptions (friendship-formation timing versus behaviour-change timing) —
    and critics (Aral and colleagues) showed that plausible selection dynamics can generate the
    same patterns. Centola's experiments cut through with design: a large online community where
    joining a health forum was randomly seeded — exposure cascades in clustered networks, not
    random ones. His follow-up discovered <strong>complex contagion</strong>: unlike diseases
    (simple contagion — one exposure suffices), behaviours with social risk or effort (joining a
    protest, adopting a health behaviour) need <em>multiple</em> reinforcing exposures from distinct
    sources, which reverses the optimal network shape — wide bridges that carry information well
    can be too narrow to carry behaviour. The mature position: information spreads along weak ties,
    behaviour spreads through redundant reinforcement, and any claim of "social contagion" must
    first survive the homophily test.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="simple or complex contagion?" widget="sort-buckets" config={netContagionSort} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "echo chambers make everyone's feeds identical echo chambers."
    Empirical work is more nuanced: algorithmic recommendation can narrow exposure, but measured
    ideological segregation online is substantially a pre-existing feature of whom people choose to
    connect with, varies enormously by platform and topic, and in some datasets exceeds what
    choice-based models predict offline. The network-structure facts are solid; the causal
    responsibility of the algorithm is genuinely contested — a live research fight, not a settled
    headline.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — the echo-chamber claim" widget="judge" config={netEchoJudge} />

  <h2>Social capital: bonding, bridging, and the plumbing of inequality</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Social capital</strong> — the resources embedded in relationships — has structural
    variants with different payoffs. <em>Bonding</em> capital (dense, closed, similar networks,
    Coleman's closure) delivers support, trust enforcement and norms; it is what families and
    tight communities provide, and closure is why dense networks can also suffocate (the same
    closure that enforces norms enforces surveillance). <em>Bridging</em> capital (spanning,
    heterogeneous ties, Burt's brokerage across <strong>structural holes</strong>) delivers novel
    information and opportunity; Burt's organisational studies found brokers — people whose
    networks span disconnected groups — consistently promoted faster and paid more, holding
    everything else constant. Putnam's <em>Bowling Alone</em> documented the aggregate decline of
    US civic connectedness late 20th century and tied it to civic outcomes; the bonding-bridging
    distinction explains why its prescriptions are hard (the policies that build bonding capital
    are not those that build bridging).
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="drag between bonding and bridging" widget="compare" config={netCapitalCompare} />
  <p>
    Now the inequality payoff. Jobs, information and norms flow through ties; therefore the
    <em>structure</em> of who knows whom — not just who intends what — reproduces stratification.
    DiTomaso's interviews with white American job-holders found advantage operating through
    <strong>favoritism rather than exclusion</strong>: people helped those in their networks, and
    networks had been built along lines of prior advantage — inequality perpetuated by ordinary
    in-group helping, with no bigot required. Add small-world mathematics (a segregated network
    is a small world <em>within</em> each group and a large world <em>across</em> groups) and you
    get the modern understanding of opportunity hoarding: the plumbing, not just the pump, carries
    the water where it was laid.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the faulty line — the inequality plumbing" widget="spot-error" config={netPlumbingSpot} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "reproducing inequality through networks requires intent —
  someone doing the excluding." It feels reasonable because favouritism is visible and looks
  deliberate. But DiTomaso's finding is that advantage flows through ordinary in-group helping:
  people assist those they know, and networks were built along lines of prior advantage. The
  correct picture is a segregated small world — dense within groups, sparse across them — so
  opportunity reproduces itself through the plumbing of who knows whom, with no bigot required
  and no gatekeeper intending anything.</p>

  <h2>Measuring networks: data, methods and ethics</h2>
  <p>
    Network data come with their own craft and their own ethics. <strong>Collection</strong>
    methods each bias differently: name generators ("who do you discuss important matters
    with?" — the General Social Survey item whose declining modal answer of zero sparked the
    discussion-portfolio literature), rosters (check your friends from a list — higher recall,
    bounded), trace data (email, calls, platform interactions — behavioural, massive, and
    measuring interaction rather than intimacy), and observation (the ethnographic route into
    meaning). <strong>Sampling</strong> networks is genuinely hard: random node samples lose
    the structure; snowball and respondent-driven sampling climb ties (the standard for hidden
    populations, with estimators correcting the bias); whole-network studies trade
    generalisability for completeness. The statistics are equally specialised — network
    autocorrelation models, stochastic block models, temporal networks for ties that form and
    dissolve — because a network is a random object, not a table. And the ethics are
    distinctive: a "de-identified" network position can identify a person (your pattern of
    ties is a fingerprint — the AOL and Netflix dataset re-identification lessons), consent is
    ambiguous at the edge (both endpoints of a tie are stakeholders), and platform-scale data
    raise the dual-use questions the Cambridge Analytica episode made public.
  </p>

  <h2>Networks inside organisations and markets</h2>
  <p>
    The organisational applications are the method's quiet commercial success.
    <strong>Advice and trust networks</strong> inside firms predict performance better than
    org charts — the informal structure does the work; <strong>brokerage careers</strong>
    (Burt's compensation findings) and team-composition research (weak-tie diversity
    predicting innovation metrics in patent and software data) quantify the structural
    advantage; <strong>segregation by network measures</strong> (homophily in hiring
    referrals) gives workplace inequality's plumbing its operational variables. In markets,
    network position prices assets: banks' interconnectedness predicts systemic risk (the
    2008 counterparty networks); buyer-supplier networks propagate shocks (the semiconductor
    shortage's amplification); and collective-accuracy mechanisms (prediction markets,
    wisdom-of-crowds with independence corrections) run on the same insight — aggregate
    accuracy depends on the network's independence structure. The general lesson: wherever
    flows matter — information, money, disease, influence — the topology is a first-order
    variable, and the tools above measure it.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In your own words: what makes a tie "weak", and why did that weakness carry Granovetter's strongest empirical finding?</li>
    <li>A public-health team wants a new health behaviour to take hold across a community. Should they seed it through wide bridges reaching many distinct people, or through clustered groups with redundant exposure? Justify with complex contagion.</li>
    <li>Why does homophily make Christakis and Fowler's obesity-contagion claims hard to establish?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Strength is structural, not emotional: a weak tie is an
    acquaintance outside your cluster, hence a bridge to information your close ties cannot
    reach — Granovetter's job-seekers mostly found work through contacts they rarely saw.
    (2) The clustered groups: behaviours with social risk or effort need multiple reinforcing
    exposures from distinct sources, so redundancy beats reach — the reverse of simple
    contagion such as disease. (3) Similar people share environments and trajectories, so
    co-trending friends are expected with zero influence; only randomised exposure (Centola's
    designs) or strong temporal assumptions can separate selection from contagion.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Network structure is a cause in its own right, measurable with degree, clustering, betweenness, eigenvector centrality and bridges.</li>
    <li>Weak ties carry novel information because strong ties are informationally redundant.</li>
    <li>Small worlds need only a few rewired long-range links: high clustering and short paths coexist.</li>
    <li>Contagion claims must first survive the homophily test; complex contagion needs redundant reinforcement.</li>
    <li>Bonding capital enforces norms; bridging capital delivers opportunity — brokers profit from structural holes.</li>
    <li>Advantage flows through networks by favoritism, so unequal ties reproduce unequal outcomes without intent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The natural next step in this subject is <em>Globalisation and Society</em>, which scales
    these tie patterns to trade, migration and institutions spanning the planet. If the
    measurement side pulled at you, the statistics ladder's network problems — interference,
    block models, temporal ties — are where that craft deepens.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Causal inference when everyone interferes with everyone.</strong> Standard statistics
    assumes one person's treatment cannot affect another's outcome (SUTVA); networks violate this
    by design — vaccination protects contacts, a friend's adoption changes your adoption. The
    toolkit that emerged is now shared across epidemiology and social science: cluster randomisation
    and two-stage randomisation (randomise seeds, let diffusion happen — Centola's designs),
    exposure mapping, and interference-aware estimands. <strong>Epidemics on networks.</strong>
    Reproduction number R is a population average over a degree distribution: on a scale-free
    network the hub structure changes the threshold logic (heterogeneous networks are easier to
    infect and to protect — targeted vaccination of hubs beats random vaccination orders of
    magnitude in efficiency, the reason ring and contact-based strategies exist). <strong>Platform
    networks as engineered environments.</strong> Feeds, friend suggestions and ranking are
    interventions on tie formation at civilisational scale, mostly unexperimented-upon; a handful
    of internal experiments (the 2012 Facebook emotional-contagion study and its ethics firestorm;
    turnout-mobilisation experiments that measurably shifted millions of voters) mark the research
    frontier and its governance problems. <strong>Open questions:</strong> how much of social-
    media segregation is choice versus design (requires platform data access researchers mostly
    lack); whether complex contagion scales to political behaviour at population scale; how
    generative-AI agents — synthetic accounts that pass as ties — will corrupt network measurement
    itself; and whether the field can finally integrate the two halves Granovetter joined: formal
    structure with the meanings people give their ties.
  </p>
  <p>
    A closing note connects this chapter to the atlas's wider argument: networks are where
    method and meaning meet. The formal measures are meaningless without the cultural content
    of ties — what a "friend" is, what obligation a favour carries — and the cultural content
    is unreadable without the structure (who is connected to whom constrains what meanings can
    travel). The strongest contemporary work holds both: structure measured carefully, meaning
    studied seriously, and the causal-identification problems named rather than laundered.
    That dual literacy — formal and interpretive at once — is the transferable skill this
    subfield offers the rest of social science, and the reason network analysis has migrated
    so successfully into epidemiology, economics and organisational research: the pipe and
    the water both matter.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="drag between the pipe and the water" widget="compare" config={netPipeCompare} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: weak ties, small worlds, homophily, brokerage" widget="quiz" config={netQuiz} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> every question forces the identification move this chapter
    taught — separating contagion from homophily, brokerage from raw degree. If you miss one,
    read the explanation before moving on: the wrong options are the plausible-sounding
    mistakes, not straw men.
  </p>

